            0.496,
            0.042,
            0.843,
            0.059
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Earth as a System: Energy, Matter, and Life"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.856,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 259"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.083,
            0.775,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The atmosphere is continuously modified by natural processes, influencing weather, climate and energy balance. When solar radiation and cosmic rays interact with the atmosphere, they influence weather and climate, and affect communication systems. Thus, atmosphere makes the Earth a unique planet."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.171,
            0.182,
            0.207
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.115,
                0.171,
                0.182,
                0.207
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_7_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.178,
            0.41,
            0.196
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Threads of Curiosity"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.211,
            0.467,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Why is the ozone layer so important?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.141,
            0.23,
            0.933,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ozone layer is vital to life on the Earth because it acts as a protective shield, absorbing harmful UV radiation from the Sun. When ozone molecules are destroyed faster than they are naturally formed, the layer becomes thinner and less effective. In the late 20th century, human-made chemicals called chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), used in refrigerators and aerosols, caused severe ozone loss over Antarctica, which came to be known as the ozone hole. Increased UV radiation on the Earth can harm the living organisms and the ecosystems. A global agreement known as the Montreal Protocol reduced the use of CFCs and the ozone layer is now slowly recovering, showing the power of international scientific cooperation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.374,
            0.688,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.2 Uneven Heating Causes Wind and Ocean Currents"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.422,
            0.776,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have learnt that wind is the movement of air from a region of high pressure to a region of low pressure. These pressure differences are mainly caused by the uneven heating of the Earth’s surface by the Sun. Uneven heating of land and water is also responsible for phenomena, such as land and sea breezes, which you have studied earlier. The same basic idea helps us understand the formation of winds and ocean currents on vastly different scales."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.785,
            0.426,
            0.859,
            0.463
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Grade 8",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Curiosity "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 6",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.55,
            0.316,
            0.568
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "13.2.1 Local winds"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.572,
            0.662,
            0.725
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Uneven heating of the Earth’s surface also produces "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "local winds",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", such as valley and mountain breezes. In mountainous regions, the slopes and the valley floor do not heat up and cool down at the same rate. During the day, the mountain slopes facing the Sun are heated more rapidly than the valley floor. As a result, the air over the slopes becomes warm and rises, creating a low pressure region. Cooler air from the valley moves up the slopes to replace the rising warm air. This flow of air is called a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "valley breeze",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 13.8a)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.107,
            0.727,
            0.664,
            0.949
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "After sunset, the situation reverses. The mountain slopes lose heat faster and become cooler, while the valley floor remains relatively warmer. The air over the slopes becomes cooler and denser, and flows down into the valley. This is known as a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "mountain breeze",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (Fig. 13.8b). Such daily changes in the direction of the wind are commonly experienced in hilly regions like Shimla, Dehradun, and other Himalayan valleys. These local winds influence the weather conditions, agriculture and daily life in mountainous regions. They help regulate temperature, moisture conditions and support soil and crop health. Thus, uneven heating of the Earth’s surface sets up winds and forms an important part of the Earth’s atmospheric circulation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
